---This resource represents a long-running operation that is the result of a network API call.
---@class gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.Operation
---@field name? string The server-assigned name, which is only unique within the same service that originally returns it. If you use the default HTTP mapping, the `name` should be a resource name ending with `operations/{unique_id}`.
---@field response? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.DocumentMap The normal, successful response of the operation. If the original method returns no data on success, such as `Delete`, the response is `google.protobuf.Empty`. If the original method is standard `Get`/`Create`/`Update`, the response should be the resource. For other methods, the response should have the type `XxxResponse`, where `Xxx` is the original method name. For example, if the original method name is `TakeSnapshot()`, the inferred response type is `TakeSnapshotResponse`.
---@field error? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.Status The error result of the operation in case of failure or cancellation.
---@field metadata? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.DocumentMap Service-specific metadata associated with the operation. It typically contains progress information and common metadata such as create time. Some services might not provide such metadata. Any method that returns a long-running operation should document the metadata type, if any.
---@field done? boolean If the value is `false`, it means the operation is still in progress. If `true`, the operation is completed, and either `error` or `response` is available.

---Represents a whole or partial calendar date, such as a birthday. The time of day and time zone are either specified elsewhere or are insignificant. The date is relative to the Gregorian Calendar. This can represent one of the following: * A full date, with non-zero year, month, and day values. * A month and day, with a zero year (for example, an anniversary). * A year on its own, with a zero month and a zero day. * A year and month, with a zero day (for example, a credit card expiration date). Related types: * google.type.TimeOfDay * google.type.DateTime * google.protobuf.Timestamp
---@class gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.Osconfig_Date
---@field day? number Day of a month. Must be from 1 to 31 and valid for the year and month, or 0 to specify a year by itself or a year and month where the day isn't significant.
---@field year? number Year of the date. Must be from 1 to 9999, or 0 to specify a date without a year.
---@field month? number Month of a year. Must be from 1 to 12, or 0 to specify a year without a month and day.

---Patch configuration specifications. Contains details on how to apply the patch(es) to a VM instance.
---@class gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.PatchConfig
---@field rebootConfig? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.PatchConfigRebootConfigEnum|string Post-patch reboot settings.
---@field goo? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.GooSettings Goo update settings. Use this setting to override the default `goo` patch rules.
---@field yum? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.YumSettings Yum update settings. Use this setting to override the default `yum` patch rules.
---@field preStep? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.ExecStep The `ExecStep` to run before the patch update.
---@field postStep? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.ExecStep The `ExecStep` to run after the patch update.
---@field skipUnpatchableVms? boolean Optional. Enables enhanced reporting for the patch job: 1. The patch job skips instances that cannot be patched and reports them as `SKIPPED`. An instance cannot be patched for two reasons: 1. The instance runs Container-Optimized OS (COS), which cannot be patched. 2. The instance is part of a managed instance group (MIG), and patching MIG instances is disabled in the patch job's configuration (PatchConfig.migInstancesAllowed is `false`). 2. The patch job is reported as `SUCCEEDED` if it completes without errors, even if some instances are `SKIPPED`. 3. The patch job is reported as `COMPLETED_WITH_INACTIVE_VMS` if it completes without errors, but does not patch instances that are `INACTIVE`.
---@field apt? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.AptSettings Apt update settings. Use this setting to override the default `apt` patch rules.
---@field windowsUpdate? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.WindowsUpdateSettings Windows update settings. Use this override the default windows patch rules.
---@field migInstancesAllowed? boolean Allows the patch job to run on Managed instance groups (MIGs).
---@field zypper? gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.ZypperSettings Zypper update settings. Use this setting to override the default `zypper` patch rules.

---@alias gcp.unstable.osconfig_v1.PatchConfigRebootConfigEnum "REBOOT_CONFIG_UNSPECIFIED"|"DEFAULT"|"ALWAYS"|"NEVER"

---Patch deployments are configurations that individual patch jobs use to complete a patch. These configurations include instance filter, package repository settings, and a schedule. For more information about creating and managing patch deployments, see [Scheduling patch jobs](https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/os-patch-management/schedule-patch-jobs).
